sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Uniform, symmetrical growth of a flattened disc does not imply uniform growth of its cells: after a quadrant divides into triangular and quadrilateral halves, the triangular cell gains area faster and reaches division size first (at about 1.345 times the radius, against about 1.517 for the quadrilateral).";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Uniform growth of a disc makes its daughter cells divide at different times";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "after bisecting a quadrant: triangular cell reaches division size at 1.345r, quadrilateral at 1.517r";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Uniform growth and unequal cell division”. Scope: flattened discoid cell aggregates growing uniformly, such as Erythrotrichia"
}
